<ArticleLayout title="Trump Accounts: the independent family guide" description="Understand who can open an account, who may qualify for the $1,000 pilot contribution, how contributions work, and what changes at age 18." sourceIds={['IRS-TRUMP-ACCOUNTS','IRS-FORM-4547-INSTRUCTIONS','IRS-REV-PROC-2026-25']}>
  <div class="callout"><strong>Key distinction:</strong> eligibility to open an account is broader than eligibility for the federal $1,000 pilot contribution.</div>
  <h2>What a Trump Account is</h2>
  <p>A Trump Account is a type of traditional individual retirement account owned by the child. During the “growth period”—through December 31 of the year before the child turns 18—special contribution, investment, and distribution rules apply.</p>
  <h2>Who can have an account opened</h2>
  <p>Generally, the child must be under age 18 at the end of the election year, have a valid Social Security number issued before the election, and not already have an initial Trump Account election filed on their behalf.</p>
  <p>For an account-only election, the IRS lists the legal guardian, parent, adult sibling, or grandparent in that priority order as possible authorized individuals. Different rules apply when requesting the pilot contribution.</p>
  <h2>Who may receive the $1,000 pilot contribution</h2>
  <p>In addition to meeting the account requirements, the child must be a U.S. citizen, have been born from January 1, 2025 through December 31, 2028, and not have had a prior pilot election processed. The person making that election must anticipate that the child will be their qualifying child for that year.</p>
  <p><a class="button" href="/tools/trump-account-eligibility-checker/">Check both eligibility paths</a></p>
  <h2>Contributions and investments</h2>
  <p>For 2026 and 2027, most non-pilot contributions—including qualifying employer contributions—share a $5,000 annual limit. Section 128 employer contributions have a separate $2,500 per-employee ceiling within that total. The $1,000 pilot amount, qualified general contributions, and qualified rollovers are excluded from the $5,000 limit.</p>
  <p>During the growth period, investments are generally limited to qualifying mutual funds or ETFs that track indexes made up primarily of U.S. companies.</p>
  <h2>Withdrawals and the age-18 transition</h2>
  <p>Distributions are tightly limited during the growth period. Starting January 1 of the year the child turns 18, most traditional IRA rules generally apply. Early distributions may be taxable and may face an additional 10% tax unless an exception applies.</p>
